# z88dk z80asm — sanitizer-instrumented file-input CLI (SPEC §6.2 item 11).
#
# Input delivery is the known-green RAW shape: a bare `@@`, no `filepath:`, no `cwd:`, no env.
# Mayhem stages the input in the run's own writable cwd and `-b` drops its scratch .o/.bin next to
# it, so the target needs no writable path of its own. Deliberately NOT redirected to a shared
# scratch dir with `-O`: `-b` writes the object file and then reads it back to link, so a fixed
# output directory would let concurrent fuzz workers clobber each other's intermediate .o.
#
# What failed run #4 ("the target times out on every test case ... and on the default test case")
# was NOT this file — it was LeakSanitizer dying under Mayhem's tracer on every exec; see
# mayhem/lsan_off.cc. The two knobs below are the smaller, related cleanups:
#
#   no MFUZZ_COMPAT_LEVEL — it was pinned at port time on the belief that a raw file-input target
#     needs it, but nothing ever asked for it here (no auto-resolution event in any of runs #1-#4,
#     and every phase reported smoketesting_succeeded). It holds mayhem-fuzz on its slowest
#     compatibility path, and all four runs sat at ~2.5 tests/s against 32-64 tests/s for the green
#     raw peers that set no env at all (dasm, chaos, microscheme, genometools). Re-introduce it only
#     if mayhem-fuzz actually reports a restart loop / return code 254.
#
#   timeout: 30 — bounds ONE exec. Pinned because run #4's own remediation text said "Increase the
#     cmd.timeout field in the Mayhemfile if needed". Insurance, not the fix.
project: savantenvs/z88dk
target: z80asm

cmds:
  - cmd: /mayhem/z80asm -b @@
    timeout: 30

# Canonical two-entry seed directive (SPEC §6.2 item 14): the server-accumulated tar plus the
# per-target committed seeds at mayhem/<target>/testsuite.
testsuite:
  - https://$MAYHEM_DOMAIN/$MAYHEM_PROJECT/$MAYHEM_TARGET/testsuite.tar
  - file://mayhem/z80asm/testsuite
